,title,link,text,language,family
0,Nilo-Saharan languages - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nilo-Saharan_languages,"The Nilo-Saharan languages are a proposed linguistic family consisting of around 210 Native African languages spoken by somewhere around 70 million speakers, mainly in the upper parts of the Chari and Nile rivers, including historic Nubia, north of where the two tributaries of the Nile meet.",Nara,Nilo-Saharan
1,Nilo-Saharan | Ethnologue Free,https://www.ethnologue.com/subgroup/39/?cf=1&token=eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiIsInR5cCI6IkpXVCJ9..qiUd4igQzzpr0OZ7p1U9JXx2uiCs003pCBFXnawm7Uo,"Nilo-Saharan. Subgroup of 210 languages.Nara nrb, a language of Eritrea. Nubian 12. Central 10.",Nara,Nilo-Saharan
2,(PDF) Nilo-Saharan Languages,https://www.researchgate.net/publication/304040925_Nilo-Saharan_Languages,"Nilo-Saharan is essentially Greenberg's earlier Macro-Sudanic (or Chari-Nile) family, together with languages and language groups formerly assigned to historically isolated units.",Nara,Nilo-Saharan
3,Nilo-Saharian languages - Sorosoro Sorosoro,https://www.sorosoro.org/en/nilo-saharian-languages/,"“Nilo-Saharan and its limits”. In Jean-Marie Hombert and Gerard Philippson (eds.), The Genetic Classification of African Languages Revisited.",Nara,Nilo-Saharan
4,"Nara people: eritrea`s nilotic ""Sky heaven"" people",https://kwekudee-tripdownmemorylane.blogspot.com/2013/08/nara-people-eritreas-nilotic-sky-heaven.html,The Nara ( formerly known as the Barya or Baria) are a Nilo-Saharan speaking people who live in Gash Barka region of Eritrea around north and east of Barentu. The Nara are small ethnic group and make up about just 1% of Erirea`s population.,Nara,Nilo-Saharan
5,Eritrea: The Nara People - Madote,http://www.madote.com/2010/04/eritrea-nara-people.html,"The Nara ( formerly known as the Baria) are a Nilo-Saharan speaking people who live in Gash Barka region of Eritrea around north and east of Barentu (2002, Chefena Hailemariam, p.75).",Nara,Nilo-Saharan
6,Nilo-Saharan Languages - SourceWatch,https://www.sourcewatch.org/index.php/Nilo-Saharan_Languages,"Nilo-Saharan Languages are a family of 205 languages. The most common are Dholuo in Kenya and Kanuri in Nigeria with 3.5 million speakers each. The next largest, measured by number of speakers are: Kalenjin; Dinka, Lugbara; Lango; Maa; Acholi; Lendu; Ngambay...",Nara,Nilo-Saharan
7,(PDF) Chabu and Kadu: two orphan branches of Nilo-Saharan,https://www.academia.edu/41113915/Chabu_and_Kadu_two_orphan_branches_of_Nilo_Saharan,"Since Nilo-Saharan was first proposed by Greenberg (1955) and its membership was expanded in a series of publications by the late Lionel Bender. One branch unknown to Greenberg, Chabu, and Kadu, which he wrongly classified as Niger-Congo were added to the canon.",Nara,Nilo-Saharan
8,Nilo-Saharan Language Family - Structure & Dialects - MustGo,https://www.mustgo.com/worldlanguages/nilo-saharan-language-family/,"The Nilo-Saharan language family includes 204 extremely diverse languages spoken by roughly 35 million people in the interior of northern Africa, including the greater Nile basin and its tributaries, as well as the central region of the Sahara desert.",Nara,Nilo-Saharan
